Backport two upstream XNU changes that current clang and iig require.

Run 40514006543 got through board_config.h and proc_reg.h and reached
~12,000 lines of log. What is left is not tissot-specific at all: two
constructs that the 8020 sources use, that clang 26 and the Xcode 26 iig
no longer accept. Both have already been changed upstream, so this patch
is a straight backport rather than a local workaround.

1. uio_stackbuf_t -> UIO_STACKBUF (bsd/sys/uio_internal.h and 14 files)

    uio_internal.h used to declare:

        typedef char __attribute__((aligned(_Alignof(union iovecs))))
            uio_stackbuf_t;

    Every user then wrote `uio_stackbuf_t uio_buf[UIO_SIZEOF(n)]`. A char
    has size 1, so that is an array whose element size is not a multiple
    of its alignment (8). clang 26 rejects it as a hard error, so all 36
    declarations across 14 files failed:

        vn.c:255:25: error: size of array element of type
        'uio_stackbuf_t' (aka 'char') (1 bytes) isn't a multiple of its
        alignment (8 bytes)

    Upstream replaced the typedef with a macro that declares a uint64_t
    array of the same byte size:

        #define UIO_STACKBUF(name, niov) \
            uint64_t name[P2ROUNDUP(UIO_SIZEOF(niov), sizeof(uint64_t)) \
                / sizeof(uint64_t)]

    8-byte elements, 8-byte alignment, same number of bytes as before --
    the declarations become `UIO_STACKBUF(uio_buf, 1);` and the call sites
    (`&uio_buf[0]`, `sizeof(uio_buf)`) keep working unchanged.

    Deliberately NOT done: enlarging the element instead of replacing the
    macro. `uio_stackbuf_t` would then be 8 bytes and every array would
    cover 8x the bytes, which is fatal for the two unbounded cases --
    uipc_syscalls.c's "1 KB" buffer would need 8 KB of a 16 KB kernel
    stack, and kpi_socket.c's msg_iovlen-sized VLA would grow to 8x a
    caller-controlled size.

    UIO_SIZEOF itself is untouched: it is also used as a byte count
    (`kalloc_data(UIO_SIZEOF(...))`), so its meaning must not change.

2. IORPC.kernelContent (iokit/IOKit/IORPC.h, iokit/DriverKit/IORPC.h)

    The iig that ships with Xcode 26 generates code for a newer IORPC
    than 8020 has, and DriverKit/OSObject.iig.cpp is generated during the
    build:

        DriverKit/OSObject.iig.cpp:277:30: error: no member named
        'kernelContent' in 'IORPC'

    Upstream added exactly that member, guarded:

        #ifdef KERNEL
                IORPCMessage     * kernelContent;
        #endif /* KERNEL */

    IORPCMessage is already declared earlier in the same header, so only
    the field needs backporting. Both copies of the header are patched
    because they are separate files with identical content, and both are
    exported (IOKit/ and DriverKit/).

